  {
   "id": "compression",
   "title": "Compression: the benchmark's gzip, framework defaults, and zstd",
   "description": "Server only: the time to compress one response body, and its size. The benchmarks above compress with `gzip.compress` at level 1 in [transports.py](transports.py). Dash compresses only with `compress=True` and Flask-Compress installed; FastAPI only with Starlette's `GZipMiddleware`. Both use the same zlib, at level 6 and level 9 by default. zstd is in the Python 3.15 standard library and is accepted by current Chrome, Edge and Firefox (`Content-Encoding: zstd`). Values are full-precision random floats, which compress less than typical real data.",
   "notes": "The benchmark's gzip level 1 costs the same as the frameworks' gzip at level 1: it is the same zlib. Their default levels are 2-27x slower for a payload at most 14% smaller. zstd level 1 is 2-11x faster than gzip level 1, and its payload 1-31% smaller. *Not part of the benchmarks above, which use gzip; measured here as a lead.*",
   "baseline": "gzip1",
   "columns": {
    "gzip1": "gzip level 1, as benchmarked (`gzip.compress`)",
    "flask-compress": "Flask-Compress default (gzip level 6)",
    "starlette": "Starlette GZipMiddleware default (gzip level 9, 64 kB chunks)",
    "zstd1": "zstd level 1",
    "zstd3": "zstd level 3 (zstd's default)"
   },
