   "id": "zstd-estimate",
   "title": "Estimate: zstd level 1 instead of gzip level 1",
   "description": "An estimate, not a benchmark run. *No compression* and *gzip level 1* are the measured end-to-end totals from sections 2 and 3. *zstd level 1* is the measured gzip level 1 total with its server compression time replaced by zstd level 1's, measured here on the same response body; the browser's decompression time is assumed the same (zstd usually decompresses faster than gzip). *Break-even* is the link speed below which compressing saves more transfer time than it costs: bytes saved divided by compression time. Browsers: Chrome and Edge accept `Content-Encoding: zstd` from version 123 and Firefox from 126; the Chromium used here sends `Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br, zstd`, also to localhost. A browser that does not offer zstd still gets gzip.",
   "notes": "Over loopback, zstd level 1 would cost mostly under 5% (up to 10%, where the measured gzip total itself ran high) against up to 19% for gzip level 1. The break-even link speed rises from 0.5-1.5 Gbit/s to 1.7-8 Gbit/s for JSON and from 23-330 Mbit/s to 0.5-5 Gbit/s for Arrow, so compressing pays off on almost any network. JSON comes out 8-31% smaller than with gzip level 1. Arrow comes out about the same size, up to 20% larger for the scale responses (random float32 values hardly compress) and 13% smaller for Dash's forecast, so zstd-compressed JSON can be smaller than zstd-compressed Arrow: Arrow's gains are in CPU time, not bytes. Serving zstd needs server support: it is in the Python 3.14+ standard library, Starlette's GZipMiddleware does gzip only, and Flask-Compress support depends on its version.",
   "baseline": "none",
   "columns": {
